**Behavior implemented:**
- Single and multi-source `mv` with correct effective-destination computation
- Invocation-wide validation (operand count, multi-source target must be existing directory) before any filesystem changes
- Per-source error handling: diagnose and continue, no rollback
- Type compatibility enforcement (dir↔nondirectory mismatches, non-empty directory targets) detected before rename
- Identical source/destination detection via inode comparison
- Directory-into-itself detection via lexical ancestor check
- Symlink handling: moves the link itself; replaces destination symlinks without following
- Arbitrary non-NUL pathname bytes preserved via `OsString`/`Path`; error messages use `to_string_lossy()`
- No prompts, no flags, no cross-filesystem fallback
- Exit status 0 on full success, 1 on any failure or invocation error
- `move_path` is the semantic anchor called directly from `main`

**Interpretation choices:**
- "Options may appear anywhere before `--`" interpreted as: any token starting with `-` (length > 1) before `--` is an unknown option and causes immediate exit 1, since checkpoint 000 has no implemented flags.
- The `is_dir()` check for deciding whether to append basename follows symlinks (matching GNU `mv` behavior), while type-compatibility checks use `symlink_metadata` (lstat) to distinguish actual directories from symlinks.
